import Foundation

extension CapabilityRegistry {
    static let chatAndTranscript: [CapabilityDefinition] = [
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .sessionSections, area: "chat list", title: "Sessions grouped into sections",
            spec:
                "The list groups rows into NEEDS YOU / WORKING / PINNED / SAVED / RECENT via groupIntoSections, dropping empty sections so no heading ever sits over nothing. What is waiting on the person — a turn stopped to ask, or work the machine found stuck — leads, longest-waiting first, because a decision must never sit below the fold; a conversation with a turn in flight leads what is merely recent in WORKING rather than being findable only by sorting on recency. Membership is decided on the (profile, session) pair, never a bare session id, and a row is listed once: a pinned conversation that is working or waiting is listed where it is working or waiting and wears its pin there, because a pin is a fact about a row rather than a place for it. Inside WORKING the order is when each conversation began, newest first, and never recency of activity: a working chat's updatedAt is a clock rather than an identity, so ordering on it makes two working chats trade places several times a second under a reader trying to click one. The section already says a row is working; where it sits must hold still for as long as it is there."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .homeBoard, area: "home", title: "Home as one ranked board",
            spec:
                "Home answers what the whole of it adds up to before it lists anything: HomeStatus.reading writes one sentence from the same rows the bands are drawn from — who is waiting on you, who is working, or that nothing is — with the counts under it (what finished today, what failed, which machine is down) and a lead light in the activity vocabulary's own tone, so it breathes, knocks or holds still like every other badge. Under it Home is one vertical list in HomeBoard's bands — NEEDS YOU, WORKING, PINNED, SAVED, RECENT, each held to HomeBandLimits so no band pushes the next off the screen, and what is waiting never capped — with nothing that scrolls sideways. Every conversation is the same row whatever band it is in: a state glyph from ActivityKind, the title, a news line only when something happened (ChatRowNews: what the agent is doing, what a waiting turn wants to do in ApprovalAsk's words, how it ended, when a machine was last heard from; a settled, read chat says nothing), then who and where. A chat that finished while the person was away says so on its own row and carries the unread dot rather than repeating itself in a section of notices. The sentence is part of the list, not the navigation title, so it survives a collapsed bar and a vertical one. The rows are content, never glass: Liquid Glass is for what floats over them."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .sessionRowStatus, area: "chat list", title: "Row state pill and glyph",
            spec:
                "Every row states what it is doing via SessionRowState: live, needs-you, offline, failed each get a pill/glyph; silence is reserved for idle. The state is resolved once in SessionRowModel from the listing and from what this device is watching first-hand (SessionPresence) — a turn running here is live before the server's next sweep agrees, and a turn that stopped for an approval says so instead of reading as merely busy."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .activityIconography, area: "chat list", title: "A busy session looks busy",
            spec:
                "Every state that is not idle has one identity across all three clients, authored once in ActivityKind: a symbol (Apple) and a one-column glyph (text), a tone drawn from four meanings — live, attention, danger, quiet — a word, a sentence for a screen reader, and a motion. The motion is part of the meaning and comes from the shared clock maths in ActivityMotion/ActivityPulse: work breathes on one slow swell, a turn stopped for the person knocks twice and rests, something being turned over sweeps, and anything settled — failed, offline, queued, idle — holds perfectly still, because stillness is how a reader tells a stopped turn from a slow one. Everything animates off the display's own clock (CADisplayLink / gtk tick callback) read as absolute time, never a counter, so every badge on screen swells in unison and one that appears mid-turn arrives in time. A frame may change light, and a sweep's one glyph; it may never change a size layout depends on. Reduced motion drops the movement and nothing else — glyph, word and colour still carry the state. One tempo, too: ActivityTuning.frameRate is what every mark on every desk runs at, and no surface animates off the vocabulary or at a rate of its own — a client that can ask its compositor for the rate does, and one that has to gate its panel's own ticks by hand asks ActivityTuning.wantsFrame rather than comparing against a bare interval, which on a 60Hz or 120Hz panel lands exactly on the boundary and silently halves the rate. Surfaces that must wear it: the chat list row (which knows only that a turn is open, so it says exactly that), and the chat's own status surface, which reads the turn down to what it is doing — the tool that is out on the machine, with that tool's own symbol, whether the answer has started arriving, a fan-out counted as agents rather than named as a call."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .backgroundWork, area: "chat list", title: "Work between turns has a face",
            spec:
                "A conversation whose agent process is still carrying work between turns — a command the model started and stepped back from, an agent it backgrounded — is not idle, and no surface may file it with everything that finished: the turn is over and the prompt is free, but the machine is still working for this chat and the agent will speak again on its own when the work ends. The server reports it as a level rather than as edges (claude-bridge stamps backgroundTasks/backgroundTask on the session, its summary and its revision, and sends a background frame on the stream the moment the CLI's own background_tasks_changed says so; the Kit carries it as BackgroundWork on AgentSession, SessionRevision, TranscriptSnapshot and ConversationState), so a missed frame can never leave a stale indicator standing, and Core gives it one face everywhere: ActivityKind.inBackground(tasks:) — live tone, breathing like the work it is, its own symbol and glyph, the count in its words — worn by the chat row (SessionRowState.background keeps the row in LIVE NOW and says BACKGROUND rather than LIVE, with the task in the agent's own words as the row's snippet), by a chat this device is streaming (SessionPresence.background, so the row and the pane agree), by the chat's own status surface (StatusFacts.Phase.background, and the segment reads the count), by the orb as one breathing satellite, and by a screen reader in a sentence that says the agent continues on its own when the work ends. The composer stays open throughout, because the CLI accepts a prompt while it carries work, and a running turn outranks it, because a turn is the louder fact. An ambient monitor a plugin runs for the life of the process is not work anybody is waiting on and is never counted. A backend with no such notion never reports one and the face never appears; nothing is inferred from a process this device cannot see."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .backgroundStop, area: "chat list", title: "Stuck work is named, and can be ended",
            spec:
                "Work between turns is not always going to finish. The harness ends nothing once the budget the model gave a command has passed — it moves the command to the background and moves on — so a shell blocked on a stdin nobody will write, or a poll loop that never meets its condition, sits there for a day while the row reads BACKGROUND and the process it holds is never reaped. The server is the witness that can tell: claude-bridge reads every background shell against the machine (its process tree's CPU time and the bytes in its output file), and a shell past its budget whose reading has not moved for a whole window is stalled. It says so — backgroundStalled beside backgroundTasks on the session, the summary, the revision and the background frame, and backgroundSince, when the oldest task began, on all four — and by default ends the shell itself (BRIDGE_STALL_WINDOW=0 turns that off), writing why into the task's own output file, the one place the harness tells the model to look, and a line into the chat for the person. The Kit carries both on BackgroundWork (since, stalled) and offers the person's own end: CodingAgentBackend.stopBackgroundWork, gated by BackendCapabilities.supportsBackgroundStop, which the bridge answers with a sentence when there is nothing it can end (a turn is open, no process is resident, the work is an agent rather than a shell) and the client shows that sentence rather than a code. Core gives the state one face — ActivityKind.stalled and SessionRowState.stalled: attention tone, held still, the pill reading STALLED, still in LIVE NOW because the process is holding the chat; SessionPresence.stalled so the pane and the row agree; StatusFacts.Phase.stalled in the chat's own band — and every row carrying work says how long the machine has been at it (SessionRowModel.backgroundSnippet: the task, then 'for 1h'), because the clock is what tells a build a minute old from a shell that has sat there since morning. Every client offers the end on a row that carries work, stuck or not: a swipe action on the phone, a context-menu item on the Mac, a row-menu entry and the driver verb stopwork on Linux, each named stopBackgroundWork, and each showing the server's refusal in its own words."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .sessionPinning, area: "chat list", title: "Pinned chats lead the list",
            spec:
                "A chat pins to the top of the list on this device (SessionPinStore, ordered). Pinned rows lead in a PINNED section ahead of every other section, keep their state pill, and pin order is the order the pins were made; unpinning returns the row to recency order. A row action pins and unpins. A server that keeps marks (sessionMarks) holds the pin and when it was made, so the order is the same on every desk; a server that does not leaves the pin on the device."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .unreadTracking, area: "chat list", title: "Unread markers",
            spec:
                "Background activity marks a session unread (SessionSeenStore); opening it clears the mark, and a chat held open in front of the reader stays marked read as it grows; a row action toggles read/unread by hand. A server that keeps marks (sessionMarks) holds the read mark in its own clock, so a chat read on the phone is read at the desk."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .savedChats, area: "chat list", title: "Saved chats with full snapshot",
            spec:
                "Bookmarks carry their own copy of everything needed to list and explain themselves when the server is unreachable or the session deleted (SavedChatStore), so the list is drawn without asking anybody."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .savedChatSync, area: "chat list", title: "Bookmarks follow the conversation",
            spec:
                "A bookmark is a fact about a conversation rather than about the device that made it — a chat saved from the couch is what a person goes looking for at the desk an hour later — so the server that holds the transcript holds the mark (claude-bridge: saved on the session summary, PATCH /sessions/:id {saved}), and every client reading that listing shows the same shortlist. The device's own copy stays whole, because the point of a saved chat is to still find it when its server is asleep. A press answers instantly and may be made with no server in reach, so it never waits on the wire: it leaves a PendingSaveIntent behind, that intent outranks the listing until it is delivered, and SavedChatSync.drain delivers it on the next listing — a server with no notion of a bookmark (opencode) retires the intent unsent and keeps the mark where it has always been, on the device, and a bridge too old for the field answers a permanent refusal, which reads the same way. SavedChatStore.reconcile then adopts the server's truth for everything nobody is holding an intent about, so a bookmark dropped on another machine goes here too."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .sessionMarks, area: "chat list", title: "Read marks, pins and the archive follow the person",
            spec:
                "What a person decides about a conversation — which chats they have read, pinned, filed away or saved — is a fact about the conversation, so the server that holds the transcript holds the decision and every client reads one answer over the tailnet, the Linux desk included; nothing about it depends on an account of Apple's or anyone's. The bridge keeps a marks ledger beside its store (claude-bridge 1.14, omp-bridge 0.8) and stamps saved, pinned, pinnedAt, archived and readAt on every summary, so a change reaches every connected client as an ordinary list push within a second. A bridge that speaks marks always says pinned and archived, false included: AgentSession.reportsMarks is how a client tells not pinned from this server cannot say, and a server that cannot (opencode, an older bridge) leaves the mark on the device. The read mark is the time of the person's last look in the bridge's own clock — the one updatedAt is read on — so a skewed phone can never make a chat read-but-unread; a press says seen or unread and the bridge stamps it. A press answers from the device at once and may be made with no server in reach, so it leaves a MarkIntent behind (MarkIntentStore); the intent outranks a listing until delivered and for a few seconds after (a listing asked for before the server heard it can land after), SessionMarkSync delivers it a second after the last press and again whenever a listing lands, each carrying the time the person decided, and the bridge weighs every field by that time rather than by arrival, so a phone back from a drawer cannot overrule a newer decision from the desk. SessionMarks.reconcile adopts what a server reports for everything nobody is holding an intent about, from fresh listings and stream pushes only — never from a cache, which describes an hour ago and would undo a press delivered since. A chat held open in front of the reader is marked read as it grows (at most every couple of seconds, and once when its turn ends), because one left unmarked until the reader walks away shows as unread on every other desk while its answer streams. A server that begins reporting marks — a bridge just updated, or restarted onto a build that has them — answers not pinned for every chat it has never heard of, so the first time a profile reports, the pins and archives this device made meanwhile are offered to it (SessionMarks.offerLocalDecisions: the pin at the moment it was made, the archive before any decision, each weighed by the bridge against anything it was told since), and a pin or archive made on a server that cannot keep marks says so, once per server (SessionMarks.deviceOnlyNotice), because silence reads as a sync that does not work."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .archivedChats, area: "chat list", title: "Device-local archive",
            spec:
                "Archiving hides a session from the main list without touching the server (ArchivedChatStore); an archived view lists, restores, and explains them. It files what is over, and never hides what is still going: a turn running, a question waiting, or work the process is carrying between turns keeps the row in the list whatever the archive says (ChatArchiveRule — one reading for all three clients, taken from what was last observed rather than from the offline mark, so a server that missed a listing cannot make the archive swallow a chat that was working). A server that keeps marks (sessionMarks) holds the archive, so a chat filed on one desk is filed on every desk; one that does not leaves it on the device, where a chat hidden while it works would be a chat that machine lost and every other still shows. Filing one says so (ChatArchiveRule.word), because on a desktop it is a single keystroke and a row that leaves the list without a word reads as a lost conversation — including when the chat stays listed because it is still working. The way back in is named with what is behind it (ChatArchiveRule.door) and reachable without scrolling — the foot of a list three hundred chats long is not a place anybody arrives at, so Linux carries the door in the sidebar menu, the Mac in its menu bar, iOS in the list's own chrome — and it is offered only while the listing actually holds something filed, counted over the listing rather than the store, since a key left behind by a deleted chat is not something to go and look at."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .deleteSession, area: "chat list", title: "Delete with optimistic removal",
            spec:
                "Deleting removes the row immediately and tells the server after; a failure resurfaces the row honestly rather than pretending."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .renameSession, area: "chat list", title: "Rename a session",
            spec: "A row action renames the session on the server and updates the row in place."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .forkSession, area: "chat list", title: "Fork a session",
            spec: "A row action forks the conversation server-side and opens or reveals the copy."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .listFilter, area: "chat list", title: "Filter the list by text",
            spec: "Typing filters rows by title/project/server without losing section structure."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .transcriptSearch, area: "chat list", title: "Search inside every conversation",
            spec:
                "The filter matches titles; this searches what was actually said. Submitting the query runs TranscriptSearch.run across every connected server at once — the bridge reads the CLI's own transcripts on its own machine (GET /search), so prose, the model's reasoning, the command a tool ran and what it answered are all findable, and a conversation's subagents are searched as part of it rather than as chats of their own — and the answers merge into one ranked list rather than a pile per server: a title that says the words first, then a conversation with the words in it, then recency. Each row names the chat, its project and server, and quotes the places it matched with the register each was in (answer/you/thinking/output/tool name), and says how many more places matched. Coverage is stated, never implied: a backend that cannot search inside is matched on the titles this device holds and labelled as such, a server that stopped early or never answered is named in TranscriptSearch.caveat, and an empty result therefore means nothing was found rather than nothing was looked at. Opening a result opens that conversation on its own server; leaving the results returns the list exactly as it was."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .autoOpenLastSession, area: "chat list", title: "Reopen the last chat on launch",
            spec: "Launch restores the previously open conversation instead of an empty pane."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .liveListUpdates, area: "chat list", title: "List streams while visible",
            spec:
                "The listing refreshes from SessionListStreaming pushes while the list is on screen; no manual refresh is the only path."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .rowContextActions, area: "chat list", title: "Per-row action menu",
            spec:
                "Long-press/right-click on a row offers save, archive, rename, fork, delete, read/unread — the same verbs on every platform."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .rowSnippet, area: "chat list", title: "A busy row names the work",
            spec:
                "While a session is working, the row's second line carries agentTask (the task in flight) in place of the meta line, so the list says what the agent is doing rather than only that it is live; idle rows keep their meta. Filtering matches the snippet too."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .rowFacets, area: "chat list", title: "The row's second line is read, not parsed",
            spec:
                "A row's second line is three facts at three weights rather than four at one: the project it is in leads, the machine and agent follow a step quieter, and the age is pinned in a fixed column at the trailing edge in monospaced digits, so staleness can be compared straight down the list. What the whole listing already says is not repeated on every row — ChatListVocabulary, computed once from the full listing, drops the server name when one machine answered and the agent name when one backend did, and a row left with nothing to say falls back to naming its server. Every client reads SessionRowModel.facets(_:) and decides only how the two weights look."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .modelIdentityTint, area: "chat list", title: "A model wears its colour",
            spec:
                "Everywhere a session's model is named — the chat list row, the composer's model chip, the effort control — the family name wears its authored hue (ModelTint, corrected in OKLab against the canvas it sits on, the same walk the palettes take) and the effort word wears its heat: cold slate for low, teal, amber, orange, vermilion for max, with ultracode outside the scale in per-letter rainbow held to the contrast floor. Both facts stay printed words — the colour is on top, never instead — and a model outside the known families keeps the quiet register rather than being issued an identity. One catalog answers every client; a family's hue may never differ between desks."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .usageGauges, area: "chat list", title: "Usage quota gauges",
            spec:
                "Account quota (usageQuota + additionalUsageQuotas) is visible at a glance from the list surface and refreshes on a slow poll. The numbers are the account's, never a machine's: every connected server's report is folded by QuotaRollup into one heading per provider — the strictest reading of each window, a provider-stated reset over a guessed one, windows only one server knows kept — and no gauge wears a hostname. A machine is named only where it is not redundant: the details surface names the servers behind a provider's numbers when more than one answered, and says nothing about hosts when one did. What the glance shows is the person's board (QuotaBoardPreferences): a provider hidden there is left out of the strip, the Home cards and the details alike, while a wall in front of the chat's own model still speaks in the chat, because that is a fact about the next send rather than a card. What the board keeps is answered for: the strip gives every visible provider a line, its tightest window, in the board's own order — walls first, since they are the news, then the rest with the roomiest saying it is still open — because a strip that lists only the hot ones reads as the others having gone, and one that re-ranks by pressure moves the rows under the reader every poll. A reading the server could not take is a card wearing the server's own reason and no invented gauges; a read that failed for a reason that may pass (a machine out of reach, a provider that did not answer) keeps the last good numbers, served marked CACHED with their age until they are a day old (QuotaKeeping, QuotaFreshness.keptFor), on the strip, in the widget's stored snapshot and on the Mac widget alike, while a key that expired or an account that does not exist is never masked by a cached figure; any reading older than ten minutes says how old it is (QuotaFreshness.staleAfter, the one figure the strip, the widget and the stored snapshot share); an estimate is never shown as the account's number, and a provider whose only reading is an estimate stays on the board as a line that says there is no measured reading (UsageQuota.isMeasured, QuotaReports.admitted) rather than vanishing; when several machines fail, the failure that needs a person (an expired key, a missing subscription) outranks one that was merely out of reach, and the machine named is the one that reported it. A window whose provider states both its start and its reset is drawn flat at that stated range, with no projected tick or tail; only a window that leaves its pace to be inferred wears the projection. A reading that names its account is folded by provider and account, so two machines on different accounts are two holdings, and the strip and the widget's stored snapshot keep them apart (QuotaHolding.identity) while the board's hiding and order stay the provider's; a machine that tried and failed is named with the short reason, is never the provenance of a live card, and leaves the glance with a notice rather than silence."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .quotaExhaustion, area: "sessions", title: "Used-up quota is a clear state",
            spec:
                "When a provider window is at or past full, or a turn fails because of a rate limit / quota wall: a clear surface names the provider and window, says when it resets when known, and tells the person to switch model or wait — never a raw rate-limit string alone. Live gauges at full read as \"Used up\". A one-shot notification fires the first time a window hits full. Pre-emptive notice rides the conversation chrome while the wall is up, not only after a failed send, and speaks only for the chat's own provider family — a wall on another provider stays in the gauges. Copy and classification live in QuotaSurface so every client says the same thing."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .quotaScoping, area: "sessions", title: "A wall speaks to the model it holds",
            spec:
                "A used-up window is worn where it applies and nowhere else. QuotaBinding reads each gauge's own label for the model it was scoped to (\"Weekly · Opus\" is Opus's; \"5-hour session\" is the account's), so the chat's standing notice speaks only when the wall is in front of the model that chat would send with — a scoped wall on a model this chat is not using is not news, and a chat whose model nobody can name hears the account-wide walls only. A wall also speaks only to the models its provider actually bills (QuotaBinding.bills): Claude's weekly holds Claude models, Grok's holds Grok's, a prepaid DeepSeek balance holds DeepSeek's, and a reseller whose caps are account-wide dollars across its whole catalog (opencode go) holds every hosted model whose *door a pick would take* is Go — a dual-door Grok row that would send through xAI OAuth does not wear Go's spent window, and a DeepSeek row under a Claude wall is the one notice nobody can act on and never appears. Nested alternates bill their own door. The door travels with the model rather than being guessed from its name: a chat read from its transcript or its session record carries the provider that ran the last answer (ActiveModel.selection, catalog-resolved before the door is given up), because a qwen with no door is assumed to be the reseller's and a qwen on llama-server is the machine's own. A failed turn is placed by the door it died on (ActiveModel.failedTurn, else the provider the message names — QuotaSurface.read), and a wall the chat has since moved away from says nothing at all rather than the countdown or the raw sentence: the person switched model, which is what the wall asked (FailureReading.moved), while a chat whose door nobody can name keeps hearing it. A turn that died on a local runtime has no wall to be behind and keeps its own words. What the notice stops saying, the chooser says instead: every model in the picker carries the wall in front of the door a pick would take (ModelChooserRow.wall), drawn spent — dimmed, wearing what ran out and when it returns (QuotaSurface.rowNote) — sunk under the models in its family that can still answer, counted in the chooser's summary, and echoed on the composer's own shortlist so the quick menu and the full list never disagree. A spent model stays pickable: a window resets, and refusing someone the model they came for is not the app's call. The reading is Core's; a client decides only how spent looks."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .markdownRendering, area: "transcript", title: "Markdown prose",
            spec:
                "Assistant prose renders headings (a `#` only with the space the grammar demands), emphasis, lists — numbered with `.` or `)`, task items as boxes (`- [ ]`/`- [x]` → ☐/☑) — links, inline code and fenced code from the shared grammar; never raw markdown source. A pipe table is lifted out of prose by MessageSegment.split alongside fences and drawn by its own capability (tableRendering). Prose never carries more than one blank line in a row — extra emptiness is the model exhaling, not paragraph structure."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .tableRendering, area: "transcript", title: "A table is a table",
            spec:
                "A pipe table is read once in Core (MarkdownTable) and drawn as an object with edges rather than as columns of loose text: a bordered card, a header band that is visibly not the body, a rule between them, and every other body row washed. The banding is the design rather than decoration — a reader tracking one row across eight sparse columns has nothing else to go on, and a cell that folds keeps both its lines inside its own band instead of dropping half of itself onto the row below. Every number is TableStyle's (the card's radius and outline, the header wash and its rule, the stripe, the cell air, the column gap, the trailing fade) and no client invents one; type is the ramp's (TypeRole.tableHeader is the quiet tracked label, tableKey the heavier voice, tableCell the reading). What a column *holds* is Core's too, because a client cannot be trusted to guess it twice the same way: figures share one digit width (MarkdownTable.isNumeric, a reading with its unit — `130 Mb/s`, `2437 MHz` — included), a column of addresses or hashes is code (isCode), and where a column sits is the author's own choice kept exactly as written — a colon in the delimiter row is a decision (MarkdownTable.declared) while a bare `---` is markdown's default, so a column of figures nobody placed lands under its own last digit (effectiveAlignment) and one the author pushed left stays left. A key-first table sets its first column in the heavier voice (namesItsRows), the way a tool's name outweighs its arguments. The columns are sized by shared arithmetic against the width the pane actually has rather than a per-client constant (TableLayout): each column asks for its widest cell measured in the client's own face and in points rather than characters — a character count against a proportional face hyphenated a four-letter header to `Ban-d` with half a window empty beside it — a narrow column is its own measure and never grown to the squeeze floor, a column with nothing worth breaking keeps its whole measure and the prose columns pay for the room (rigidColumns), folding happens only when it makes the table fit because a table that will scroll either way is better read short, and what still does not fit scrolls sideways inside the row with its last inch dissolving rather than clipped or dragging the pane out. A table is drawn once it is whole, because a table arriving a row at a time is a table measured a row at a time — every cell laid out again, every column re-fitted, the card's height re-negotiated — inside the one layout pass the answer beside it is also being painted in, so the cost lands on the whole window as a stutter in time with the writing. While it is being written its own card stands in its place wearing the open-work mark and the count of what has landed (TableDraft, isGrowing: it is the last segment of a message the server has not finished) and not one row is measured; the moment it is whole it is built once and washed in down its own bands (TableEntrance — a change in light, never in size, on one beat whatever the row count, and only for a table that was a draft on this screen a moment ago, because a table read out of history is a settled fact and a settled fact does not animate). The other arrival rules still hold: a row still being typed is not offered (MessageSegment.split(sealed:)), a header whose delimiter has not landed is not a line of raw pipes on the page, and a column may widen under the writing and never narrows (TableLayout.settled). A copy or export writes the pipes back (MarkdownTable.markdown), colons included."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .transcriptLinks, area: "transcript", title: "An address is touchable",
            spec:
                "Every address in the transcript opens, whether it was written as a markdown link or pasted bare into a sentence — Autolink finds the bare ones in Core (a scheme or a `www.` host, a dot inside the host, trailing sentence punctuation handed back, a closing bracket kept only when the address opened one) so all three clients agree on where a link starts and stops. A link is drawn in the theme's accent and underlined, never as prose the reader has to retype off a screen. Activating one hands it to the browser the person is already signed in to — never a webview with an empty cookie jar, because the pages worth opening from a transcript are behind an account. A page Claude published for the conversation is recognised as such (ArtifactLink) and says so where it is shown, since its address is the only evidence in the transcript that the deliverable exists."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .syntaxHighlighting, area: "transcript", title: "Code is coloured by what it is",
            spec:
                "A fenced block is lexed by SyntaxHighlighter — one shared, toolkit-free pass over ~60 language tags — and painted through SyntaxPalette, which derives every role from the theme's own slots on its own code background: keywords wear special, names wear info, strings wear warn, numbers wear accentDim, comments are textDim blended toward the canvas. A diff is read twice (DiffHighlight): its first column decides each line's ground — added and removed lines sit on a wash of the same accent and danger the diff's +N/−N labels wear (SyntaxPalette.diffLineBackground), with the marker glyph keeping the diff's full ink — and the language the patch's own headers name colours the code on every line, corrected against the wash it sits on; a patch that names no file keeps whole-line accent and danger, and a headed block's header wears both facts (`diff · swift`). Comments and strings are claimed before anything else, so a `//` inside a string is not a comment and a keyword inside a comment is not a keyword; an unterminated run claims the rest of the block, which is what a block still being streamed looks like. A block's header wears the canonical language name and an unknown fence tag keeps its own spelling and renders plain rather than being guessed at. Code scrolls horizontally and is never reflowed — code that rewraps is code you cannot read — and it never scrolls vertically inside the transcript: a scroller inside the transcript's scroller is two gestures fighting over one wheel, so a block longer than TranscriptBlocks.inlineLines shows its first lines beside a gutter of line numbers and one button naming how many are behind it (TranscriptBlocks.fold), and opening it grows the block in the page where the reader's own scroll carries it. The same fold stands under a long tool output. A copy takes the block's exact bytes and the button says *copied* for a moment where the pointer already is."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .streamingGrowth, area: "transcript", title: "Parts grow in place",
            spec:
                "A streaming part updates its existing row (reconfigure/diff), never tearing down the transcript or losing scroll position."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .streamCascade, area: "transcript", title: "The answer is written, not pasted",
            spec:
                "Streamed prose plays out of a buffer instead of appearing in whatever lumps the network delivered. LiveCascade renders the whole markdown-safe prefix once per arrival (CascadeGate holds the renderer at the last position where no inline token is half-open, so no answer ever flashes its asterisks) and then reveals through the *rendered* characters, which is what makes the speed even — punctuation the renderer ate never counts. StreamCadence plays at a rate that changes on a long time constant and may never drain the buffer faster than CadenceTuning.floorTime, so a model that pauses reads as a hand slowing rather than a frame dropping. RevealPlan reserves the rest of the word being written and lays it out invisibly, so a per-character reveal never rewraps a line — glyphs arrive in place. The last StreamCascade.span characters carry the wave: heat toward the theme's accent, a shimmer band travelling back on its own clock, and a per-glyph entry fade from StreamCascade.entryFloor. Ultracode paints the edge with the shared rainbow. Frames come from the display's own clock at up to 120Hz. New rows enter with the same easing and stagger; history, a chat switch or a burst past the tuning is adopted whole; reduced motion reveals at once; and the wave comes off by hand when it lets go of a row."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .toolRows, area: "transcript", title: "Tool call rows",
            spec:
                "Each tool call is a compact row stating tool, target and status, expandable to its payload; one row per MessagePart."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .toolDiffs, area: "transcript", title: "Edit tools render diffs",
            spec:
                "Edit/write tool calls show an added/removed line diff (ToolDiff), not raw JSON. The call's own file_path names the language (ToolDiff.language), so the lines get the same treatment as a fenced patch: the diff washes under them, the file's syntax on them."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .compactActivity, area: "transcript", title: "Agent steps collapse to a slim line",
            spec:
                "A run of thinking and tool calls collapses to a single slim line naming the run at a glance — status glyph, step count, the tools involved — instead of a full-width card, and expands in place to the same detail either way. Where the client exposes the choice, the compact collapsed form is the default."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .imageParts, area: "transcript", title: "Pictures the agent hands over",
            spec:
                "Tool results that gave the model an image become file parts docked at the tool call, fetched over /files/raw and rendered inline with a filename caption. Direction is size: a picture the agent hands over is content and fills the row's preview, while one you sent is a receipt and renders at ImagePreview's share of that box — same shape, same tap to open it full size, no caption. A client decides only what units the rule is applied in."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .imageViewer, area: "transcript", title: "Image viewer over the conversation",
            spec:
                "Tapping a picture opens a gallery over every image in the conversation — paged, zoomable to 1:1 — and any save/share hands over the server's original bytes, never a re-encode."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .subagentCards, area: "transcript", title: "Subagents inline",
            spec:
                "A subagent renders as a card docked at its spawning tool call, expanding in place; a wide fan-out collapses behind one group row; never a separate chat. The row's live line is StatusFacts.liveDetail(_:at:under:), and its clock belongs to the agent rather than to whoever is reading: a sidecar goes on claiming it is working for as long as its window lasts, so the spawning call bounds it — AgentHold(call) — and an agent under a call that is over stopped when the call stopped, at the latest. Otherwise a four-minute errand reopened a week later reads as seven days and grows again every time the transcript is drawn."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .taskBoard, area: "transcript", title: "The agent's plan is one live board",
            spec:
                "Claude keeps its to-do list by tool call — TodoWrite rewrites the whole list, TaskCreate/TaskUpdate grow it a task at a time with the number only in the result string — and TaskBoard (Core) folds every such call in transcript order into one list. The transcript shows the board once: the LAST board-moving call renders as a checklist card — done struck through, the in-progress task wearing its activeForm and the accent, pending dim, headed by \"N of M done · what is happening now\" — and every earlier board call stays the one-line tool row it was, so a long run reads as one plan updating rather than twenty snapshots. TaskList/TaskGet read the server's list and change nothing, so they stay ordinary rows. The card is the fold of the transcript the device already holds — no server route, works on saved chats."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .workflowCard, area: "transcript", title: "A workflow run is one card",
            spec:
                "A Workflow call renders as the run it started, not the receipt it returned: the workflow's name and description, the phase plan its script declares, live agent rows (what each is doing, for how long), a progress reading over the fan-out in hand, elapsed, and — when the run's task reports back — the answer folded into the same card. Phase attribution is only claimed once the finished run records it; a live card shows the plan and the agents, never a guessed position between them. A run ends because the harness said so, never because a clock ran out: the launching call answers in milliseconds and the work runs for minutes, so the only ending is the report the harness posts afterwards, carried to the client as ToolCall.background rather than paraphrased into prose the card would have to parse back. WorkflowRun.State turns that into finished, stopped or failed — a run that was killed is over without being blamed for a fault — and the card wears exactly one mark for it, run.activityIcon, so the header cannot disagree with the agent rows under it. Every ending holds perfectly still, because a record that keeps moving reads as work that never ended — the agent rows included: an agent's sidecar goes on reporting itself active for half an hour after the run around it is over, so ActivityIcon.workflowAgent(_:in:) reads the run and settles every row the moment the run settles. The phase rail answers to WorkflowRun.phaseStanding rather than to isLive: only a reported completion fills it, because nothing records which phase a run was in when it was stopped or when it broke, and a killed four-phase script drawn as four finished phases claims work the transcript never saw. Nothing about a run is measured against the reader's clock — an ending is a fact about when the work stopped, so a run whose report carried no time reports no length at all rather than one that grows every time the conversation is reopened. The rows are timed the same way, because an agent that never reported finishing has no ending of its own either: WorkflowAgent.elapsed(at:in:) reads the run too, and an agent inside a run that is over stopped when the run stopped — at the latest — so a card reopened a week later shows the errand it ran, not seven days of it."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .questionCells, area: "transcript", title: "Questions, not tool rows",
            spec:
                "An AskUserQuestion call docks as a question card at the end of the transcript and the answer goes out through the normal send path, never a direct backend call. The options an agent offers are a shortlist, not the whole of what can be said, so every card carries a line to type an answer of your own on — present on the card beside the options rather than behind a sheet or a menu, because an alternative you have to go looking for reads as absent, and the answer the agent needs is often the one it did not think to offer. On a question that takes one answer, typing takes the ticks off and the return key sends it; on one that takes several, what is typed stands beside them. It is a prompt box like any other: what is half-written into it is kept by DraftStore against the ask itself, so it survives the app being closed on the question it answers."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .designBoards, area: "transcript", title: "A design is looked at, not described",
            spec:
                "A change to a surface is argued about in pictures, so /design opens a preflight (DesignPreflight) that composes one brief — what to design, where it is now, what it must respect, how many alternatives — and sends it as an ordinary turn: the agent writes one self-contained HTML mock per alternative into .tailscode/design/<slug>/ plus a board.json naming each one, and changes no source. The brief states the convention rather than a command name, so it works on every agent this app talks to, and the word is answered by the app rather than by the server's catalog (SlashDispatch.designPreflight, CommandCatalogStore.designCommand) because what comes back is a surface this client renders. The board announces itself by that manifest being written — DesignReading watches the path, not the tool's name, since every agent calls its writer something different — and docks as a card at the call that wrote it, never a line of blue text. Opening it reads the manifest and each mock over the file route and renders them in the platform's own engine (WKWebView, WebKitGTK): one artboard at a time, full-bleed, its letter and name over it, its rationale and the annotations the agent stuck on it beside it, arrows or 1-9 to switch. Three verbs and no more: change this one (rewrites that mock in place), one more (appends an artboard), build this (the implement prompt, which says outright that the mock is a picture of the result rather than code to paste, and carries any last instruction). Every word is DesignBoardState's and every prompt is DesignFollowUp's, so a client decides only how a mock is framed. A board whose manifest cannot be read says so and offers the reread; a design skill that published to a link instead of to files is surfaced as that link rather than swallowed."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .linkEmbeds, area: "transcript", title: "An address wears its face",
            spec:
                "A link in the transcript is more than blue text: the addresses of a prose run gather on one rail, a single flat line under the run, rather than a card apiece. A run is the consecutive prose between two pieces of furniture, so a message with prose, a tool run and more prose has two rails, and a rail arrives only when its run closes — a tool row, a picture or the end of the turn follows — once, so a streaming answer never carries a rail its own growing text keeps pushing down; the address in the prose is already a link while it streams. The line is 24 pt at rest (32 pt full width on a touch screen, the visible line inside it) and the same height at every stage of its life, so a fetch landing moves nothing: up to three overlapping favicons (the host's glyph until the icon is known), the hosts in link ink joined by a middle dot, +N for the rest, and a quiet chevron; a lone address reads as its title with its host after it. A pointer client opens the rail's plate over the rows below after a short rest of the pointer — a floating list that never relays out the transcript, eight 36 pt rows then scrolling, opening upward when there is less room below than the plate is tall — and a touch client expands the rail in place into 44 pt rows with the scroll anchor held. At most twelve addresses are kept (LinkRailPolicy, Core), each once, http and https only; titles and icons are fetched for the first three at creation and for the rest the first time the rail opens, debounced and cached, a streamed address still growing fires none at all, and LinkReach still gates every fetch. It is metadata only: the fetch reads the page's head for a title and an icon, never the conversation's context. An address opens exactly the way the link itself opens — the reader's own browser, never an embed of the page — and the rail offers to copy one address or all of them. Failure of the fetch is not failure of the link: the row stays pressable with the host as its face. The rail is one disclosure button to a screen reader, who is told how many links, the first hosts and whether it is open (LinkRailReading words it once for every client), and the opened rows are links in reading order. The rail is on by default and the reader can turn it off: LinkEmbedsSetting (Core) is the one answer every client asks, off means plain links and nothing else, and flipping the switch rebuilds an open transcript at once rather than on its next turn."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .chatDensity, area: "settings", title: "Chat density",
            spec:
                "Furniture costs a line, not a card. The transcript spends its height on the answer: tool calls, thinking and subagents rest as one flat line each, compaction and the other seams as a single divider line, the pictures an agent made as one grid as wide as the row and never taller than one picture — a strip across the transcript when they are few, a two-row sheet of squares when they are many, the rest counted on the last tile's corner, and every tile opening the conversation's viewer on its own picture, paged over every picture — and the gaps between rows are set per relationship — a paragraph, prose beside furniture, prose beside code, a picture gallery, a new turn — rather than one number between everything. The numbers are one table in Core (ChatMetrics, with ChatMetrics.gap(from:to:) answering for any two neighbouring row classes) and each client maps them onto its own tokens, so no cell hard-codes a gap. Compact is the default and comfortable keeps the airier rhythm; the choice is the person's (ChatDensitySetting, one device-local setting under tailscode.chatDensity, found in Settings under Appearance), and changing it rebuilds an open transcript at once. Compaction takes padding, chrome and repetition, never glyphs: prose keeps its role, size and leading, and code keeps its lines. Nothing a person must see is folded — a pending permission, a question, a failure and an answerless turn stay open — and a pressable line keeps its target: 24 pt on a pointer, 32 pt full width on a touch screen, 44 pt for an opened row. Each client's furniture is its own: a card-less activity line, a divider seam and a picture gallery on all three, with the code block's header revealed on hover on the desktops and kept on the phone."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .neuralTrace, area: "transcript", title: "Everything an agent did is one Megatron",
            spec:
                "Everything an agent did before its final message is one Megatron, the mecha head the transcript is drawn on, not a stack of \"N tools\" lines. A turn's thinking, tool calls and mid-turn notes become nodes in a fixed anatomy (PLAN, SENSE, ACT, SHELL, DELEGATE, SAY), a shape per kind so tint is never the only signal, and the settled turn is one line: a head glyph, the count, the duration and +N −M. Wires appear only where the transcript shows a relation: one step after another, calls made together in one message, and a dashed tie where two steps touched the same file or ran the same command. The head's state is the ActivityKind the header, the orb and the rows already read, with the lit zone the tool family that activity names; hovering or focusing a node shows its card (tool, argument, outcome, first lines of output, which message, what it shares), and Rows lists every step for the screen reader and the touch path. Megatron wears its name as a small label, at the right end of the collapsed card's headline when the headline leaves it room and in the open stage's top left corner, and on a desktop the open stage keeps to 72 percent of the pane's visible height (the step list and the readout give rows up to a floor of four) so it never swallows a short window. The brain is drawn by Metal on the Apple clients and by a Core CPU compositor on Linux; nothing opens by itself, so every turn is a card until a person opens it, and only one brain is open per window. The old run rows remain as the Lines style (ActivityStyleSetting, one device-local setting under tailscode.activityStyle, Brain by default), and NeuralReading writes every word once in Core. On a phone (a touch row under 860 points, `NeuralStageMetrics.make(phone:)`) the open Megatron is compact and holds no scroller of its own: a short head, one bar for the zone chips and the search, a list of five rows that is a window around the cursor (or the newest steps while a turn runs) ending in \"N more steps\", which opens a short list where it stands and a long one's account in a sheet."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .compactionSeam, area: "transcript", title: "Compaction is a seam",
            spec:
                "A Compaction part renders as a divider stating what was traded for what, with the machine-facing summary behind a reader; /compact always passes through a preflight that warns, and takes an instruction for what the summary must keep where the server's compaction accepts one (Claude Code) — a server whose summarize takes none (opencode) gets the same preflight without the field, because a sentence the server would drop is a promise it cannot keep. opencode's wire shape for a compaction is three pieces — a marker part on an empty user message, then an assistant message whose text is the summary — and the backend folds them into the same seam every client renders: the marker drives the running activity from the event stream, and the completed summary is carried inside the Compaction part."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .permissionCards, area: "transcript", title: "Permission requests",
            spec:
                "pendingPermissions render as approval cards with the tool's ask spelled out and approve/deny (and remember, where the backend offers it)."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .failureSurface, area: "transcript", title: "Failures say so",
            spec: "lastFailure surfaces as a visible banner/row with the message, never a silent stall."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .answerlessTurn, area: "transcript", title: "A turn that said nothing says so",
            spec:
                "A turn can finish having produced nothing at all — no words, no tool call, no picture, and no error, which is what a provider refusing a request mid-stream leaves behind. Every part of the transcript is built from what a turn produced, so that outcome draws nothing anywhere: the spinner stops, the transcript sits exactly as it was, and the question reads as ignored. ChatMessage.isAnswerless names it from the message itself — assistant, completed, no error, and not one part carrying content — excluding a turn somebody stopped by hand, whose emptiness they already understand. AnswerlessTurnReading turns it into the row's words, and no client writes its own: what happened, the server's own finish word quoted when it adds anything, and — when the question carried pictures, which is the commonest thing a model is refused for holding — that fact and the remedy that follows from it. Nothing claims a cause the transcript cannot show. The row is settled, so it holds perfectly still, and it offers exactly one action: send the same words again, without the pictures where they were there, which every client wires to the ordinary send so the retry is a message like any other. The words come from the prompt in the transcript rather than from a send this device happens to remember, so the row works on a conversation opened from another machine."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .interruptedTurn, area: "transcript",
            title: "A turn the machine cut off is not a turn that was ignored",
            spec:
                "A server that stops mid-answer — updated, killed, slept, powered off — leaves a conversation indistinguishable from one where the model had nothing to say: the prompt is there, no answer follows, the spinner is gone. It is the one failure that must never render as silence, because the agent may have been three files into an edit. The server names it (TurnInterruption on ConversationState, fetched on every refetch and delivered live on the stream, so a client that was not connected when it happened still finds out), InterruptedTurnReading writes the words once in Core, and all three clients draw the same card docked at the end of the transcript: what was asked, how long the turn had run, when the stop was noticed — never why, because nobody knows — and the account of what the work had actually done, read off the agent's own transcript rather than off anything it claimed: tools counted, the one it was inside, the files it wrote to named without their paths, the commands it ran, how far the answer had got, and the prompts that were queued behind it and never ran. A turn that had done nothing says exactly that, because 'nothing on the machine changed' is the fact that decides whether starting over is safe. It is a settled state and holds perfectly still. Two actions and no more: pick it back up, which continues the work on the server that holds it, and let it go, which drops the record and touches no transcript. The card comes down on the server's answer rather than on the press, and a turn already resumed keeps the card, saying so, rather than vanishing into another silence. A backend that cannot answer the question is not a backend that answered 'nothing was interrupted' — an unsupported route leaves the question unanswered and the surface absent, never a false all-clear. The card also says what leaving it undecided costs, because it costs something nobody was being told: while an interruption stands the server will not carry that session on by itself, so an unanswered card silently turns unattended continuation off for the whole conversation. A press is answered on the card before the network can be — the button renames itself and stops taking a second press — because the server's word is a second or two away over a tailnet and may never come. And a press the server refuses is never a dead end: a conflict is the one answer that proves this device's card is out of date, so the record is re-read and the card corrects itself or comes down, and what the person is told is the server's own sentence about why, never a substitute one — a client that could only say 'the server could not pick that turn back up' was throwing away the only explanation anybody had."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .transcriptNotes, area: "transcript",
            title: "What the server wrote for the reader is a line, not a message",
            spec:
                "A server writes lines into a conversation for the person reading it as well as for the model: the model or the agent changing hands partway, a turn picked back up after the server restarted, a command or an agent the model left running reporting back, instructions loaded partway, a move to another directory, a skill taken up. The Kit carries each as a TranscriptNote part on a system message, under the id the server gave the record so a stream and a re-read agree (opencode 2's own records and descriptions; a line written only for the model, and the choices that set a conversation up before anybody wrote in it, are never notes), TranscriptNoteReading writes the sentence, symbol, glyph and tone once in Core with the model named the way the person picked it, and all three clients draw it as a quiet line across the transcript (never a bubble, never the model-facing text behind it) that holds perfectly still."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .providerRetry, area: "transcript",
            title: "A turn waiting on its provider says why",
            spec:
                "When a provider refuses or fails a request mid-turn (a rate limit, an overloaded model, a plan used up), the server waits and asks again on its own, and from the outside that wait is a model thinking very hard: the turn is open and nothing streams. The Kit carries it as TurnRetry on ConversationState (attempt, the provider's own words, when the next attempt goes, and the remedy the provider names, such as raising a limit or adding credit, with its address), set from opencode 1 and 2's retry status and schedule and cleared by any status, an answering delta or the turn's end; the wait also keeps the client's own cut-off inference from calling a long wait a dead turn. ActivityKind.retrying is its face everywhere a state is drawn (attention tone, turning), and ProviderRetryReading writes the card every client docks under the transcript: the provider's reason, which attempt failed and a countdown to the next one that wakes its clock only when the words change, and the remedy as one press that opens the provider's page. It asks nothing of the person; stopping the turn stays where it always is."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .conversationRevert, area: "transcript",
            title: "A conversation can be wound back, and the way back stays open",
            spec:
                "On a server that can (BackendCapabilities.supportsRevert: opencode 2), any message you wrote offers Undo from here: after a confirmation that says what it does, and that a running turn stops (the one part Restore cannot give back), the message and everything after it are set aside and the files the agent changed since are put back on the machine (AgentConversation.revert(to:), which stops a running turn first and asks a busy server again rather than refusing the press). While the revert stands, ConversationState.messages is the conversation before the boundary and revertedMessages holds what was set aside, so every surface reads the one in effect; the words of the message wound back to come back into the composer of the device that pressed it, ready to edit; and a banner where the set-aside messages were (RevertReading) says how many were set aside and which files changed how, with Restore one press away (restoreRevert()) until the next message is sent, which makes it final on the server and drops what was set aside from every device at once (BackendEvent.revertCommitted), never bringing it back for a moment first. The composer's words are only put back when it is empty, so a draft is never overwritten, and a Restore takes them out again only if they are still exactly what was put back. A revert standing on the server, made from another device or before the app was closed, comes back with the transcript."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .authBanner, area: "transcript", title: "Signed-out Claude is a state",
            spec:
                "A signed-out CLI shows as a warning banner in chat that leads to the sign-in flow; never tell someone to open a terminal."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .followBottom, area: "transcript", title: "Follow the bottom deliberately",
            spec:
                "The transcript follows new content only while the reader is at the bottom; scrolled up, one plain jump pill offers the way back — it counts nothing, and following resumes only from the bottom itself, never from a few lines short of it."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .transcriptFind, area: "transcript", title: "Find in conversation",
            spec: "Text search within the open conversation with match count and next/previous."),
        CapabilityDefinition(
            id: .userEcho, area: "transcript", title: "Sent words appear at once, and the ink says what became of them",
            spec:
                "A sent message renders immediately as a pending row built from what is in memory — never from a rebuild of the transcript, which is what made a long conversation swallow a send — and the row carries its phase from PendingSend. The phase is told by ink rather than by a word: a message on the wire is drawn faint (PendingSendReading.ink → .faint, opacity under one) with the words fully readable, and the colour fills in when the server has it (.full) — the transition a person watches is the bubble arriving rather than a caption changing from sending to sent under it, and neither word is ever drawn. A line appears only when the wait has become news (caption is nil until then): still sending past slowAfter, waiting for the machine to start past quietAfter — and a client wakes its clock for that moment (nextCaptionChange) rather than ticking under a row with nothing to say. A failure keeps the words at full strength in the danger tone, quotes the server's reason, and offers send-again/edit/discard. The state is spoken in words to a screen reader (PendingSendReading.state), which cannot read ink. Retired only when the server's account grows the message it stood in for."),
    ]
}
